<p>Hello!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-#### would be brought home by the field agent, who, when, in tears, hugged the object, would hear a voice calming him down.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would the Foundation let him take it home? They never let people keep anomalies.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He would later survive a stray bullet with no damage at all while he slept, hugging the plushie. After that, the field agent would bring the object to the foundation site immediately.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Where does the stray bullet come from.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>against some Groups of Interest, who would want to have SCP-#### so they could become invincible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think you need to include any GOIs, what you have so far looks good enough.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-#### would be discovered by a field agent accidentally, when a little boy hugging the object was run over by a truck and suffered no damage at all. He (the field agent) would keep an eye on the boy for some months, who seemed to have no anomalous properties.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sounds like a fun idea, try to hide the fact that the plushie is the anomaly at first then reveal it later on, you might need to include multiple iterations of the SCP.</p>
<p>Honestly, I'm liking this. I'm a huge fan of friendly SCPs and this seems to be a really good idea to include some really emotional bits if you write it well.</p>
<p>I'm gonna <span style="color: orange">Greenlight</span> this. There is room for expansion, but it still feels like a cool idea. I like how simple it is, I see a lot of new writers try to use overly complicated ideas before their ready so this looks like a really good place to start for a first time author.</p>
<p>Good luck!</p>
<p>Also, remember you <em>do not</em> need a second <span style="color: indigo">Greenlight</span> in order to ask for help on the #thecritters channel.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<em>As a courtesy to our readers on mobile devices, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em>
